chosen:
Human: When was Amazon Mechanical Turk created?

Assistant: Amazon Mechanical Turk was created in 2005 by Amazon.com Inc.

Human: Has it been profitable?

Assistant: According to Wikipedia, most studies on profitability found that the service is a "successful and profitable venture" for Amazon.

Human: Can anyone in the world become an Amazon Mechanical Turk worker or is it limited to certain countries only?

Assistant: While I'm not aware of any geographic restrictions on creating an Amazon Mechanical Turk account, there are at least a few minimum requirements that you must fulfill to become a worker:
    - your home IP address must be in one of the allowed IP address ranges;
    - you must be at least 18 years of age;
    - you have to be using a web browser (simply using an email client, such as Microsoft Outlook or Thunderbird, to access tasks will not qualify you, however);
    - particular payment methods and account information may be required depending on the country you live in;
    - your device must meet a list of minimum requirements;
    - you must register for an AWS account and verify your identity;
    - you will be provided with a unique Amazon Mechanical Turk access ID that must not be shared with anyone else.

Human: What else can you tell me about Amazon Mechanical Turk?

Assistant: In short, Amazon Mechanical Turk (AMT) is an online labor market that allows groups of people to perform simple human intelligence tasks, or HITs, for a fixed payment.  People take up these tasks, called HITs, by entering into an agreement with the requester, or using their worker account to accept a task offer made by requesters.  In most cases, AMT workers are non-contracted and can choose to take on or reject a certain task.
    People can be quite creative with AMT.  Common uses of AMT include crowd-sourced image labeling for computer vision research, data validation for software developers, survey taking, creation of scripted content for e-commerce projects, and other problems that can be made more cost-effective or efficient by taking advantage of the distributed thinking power of the crowd.  As of early 2019, users of AMT included individuals working as part-time labor, full-time workers, independent researchers, academics, executives of organizations such as financial services firms and tech companies, startups, big corporations, academic institutions, and government institutions.

